# The power key on this board asks for a suspend that will be refused.
#
# gnome-shell's power manager maps the key to power-button-action, whose default
# is 'suspend'. This package masks the sleep targets, so logind answers
# CanSuspend "no" and the request cannot succeed.
#
# The reason for that mask changed under this file and the distinction matters
# if you are re-evaluating it. Originally there were no suspend ops at all --
# Amazon's firmware is PSCI 0.2 and SYSTEM_SUSPEND is a PSCI 1.0 function, so
# psci_init_system_suspend() never called suspend_set_ops() and mem_sleep
# offered only [s2idle]. Kernel 7.2.2 fixed that with an SPM-driven suspend
# driver; mem_sleep now reads "s2idle [deep]" and a round trip completes. The
# mask stayed anyway, because a *successful* suspend still kills the MT6630
# until a reboot. See suez-no-idle-suspend.conf for the full rationale and for
# the condition under which the mask, and therefore this override, can go.
#
# So the request is refused -- but GDM has already done its pre-suspend step of
# switching to the greeter by then, which drops the user to the login screen,
# with a flash of the framebuffer console during the compositor handover, and
# nothing suspended. Measured 2026-09-01:
#
#   logind: Power key pressed short.
#   gnome-shell: POWERMANAGER: doing system action 'suspend' (reason 'power key released')
#   gnome-shell: POWERMANAGER: session active changed: false
#   logind: New session 'c233' of user 'gdm-greeter' with class 'greeter'
#
# 'interactive' gives the key the power menu instead. Note the schema has no
# 'blank' value -- the enum is nothing/suspend/hibernate/interactive -- so
# "turn the screen off" is not available here without new code.
#
# An override rather than a dconf default so it applies to the GDM greeter as
# well as the user session. It only changes the default, which is safe because
# the key is not set in either dconf database.
[org.gnome.settings-daemon.plugins.power]
power-button-action='interactive'
